  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 215 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nIn the wake of a series of upper level systems earlier this week,\na weak ridge embedded in northwesterly flow will continue to \nbring a period of warmer but breezy weather to North Central \nMontana today. This period of warmer weather will come to a quick \nend on Friday as a decaying upper level trough moves through \nSouthern British Columbia and Alberta into Montana, which will \nbring cooler temperatures, along with some showers of rain and \nmountain snowfall as it pushes through. The cooler temperatures \nwill linger behind this system, which will set up the next, \nstronger system to produce widespread precipitation with snow \nexpected above 7000 feet, which would include some of the higher \nmountain passes.The unsettled weather pattern will continue into \nearly next week, with continued chances for rain and mountain \nsnowfall.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds today: Westerly, cross barrier flow will bring some gusty \nwinds to North Central and Southwestern Montana today. While winds\nare not expected to be strong enough to reach high wind criteria \n(58 mph for most, 75 mph along the Rocky Mountain Front), gusts to\n35-50 mph will be possible across the area, especially along the \nRocky Mountain Front and north slopes of the Little Belts, where \nwinds will be strongest. \n\nMountain Snowfall this weekend: A strong upper level low will move\ninto the Treasure State over the weekend, bringing well below\naverage temperatures along with copious amounts of moisture. The\ncooler temperatures will bring snow levels down to 7000 feet above\nsea level, which will result in widespread mountain snowfall to\nareas above 7000 feet. Current forecasts keep snowfall amounts\nunder 6 inches at the moment, but some of the highest terrain\nstill have around a 10 percent chance of snowfall amounts over a\nfoot over the weekend. Ludwig",
